  <!-- Section 5: Evaluating Quotes -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">How to Evaluate and Compare Manufacturer Quotes</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Getting quotes is relatively easy - comparing them effectively is where most brands struggle. The lowest price rarely represents the best value. Manufacturers cutting corners on quality, communication, or service can offer lower unit prices that cost far more in revisions, delays, and quality problems. Effective quote comparison requires evaluating multiple dimensions beyond FOB price per unit.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Ensure Apples-to-Apples Comparison</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Before comparing prices, ensure all manufacturers quote on identical specifications. Different fabric quality, construction details, or trim specifications cause price differences unrelated to manufacturer efficiency. Provide the same tech pack, fabric requirements, and quality standards to all manufacturers. If specifications vary, request revised quotes on matching specifications. Only compare prices when the input specifications are identical.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Evaluate Communication Quality</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Communication during the quoting process predicts communication during production. Manufacturers responding quickly, answering questions thoroughly, and providing detailed information demonstrate professionalism. Slow responses, vague answers, or difficulty reaching decision-makers during quoting will not improve after you place an order. Good communication prevents misunderstandings, reduces revision rounds, and enables problem resolution. Factor communication quality heavily into your decision - it affects every aspect of the relationship.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Consider Total Landed Cost</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        FOB price is only one component of total cost. Calculate landed cost including freight, insurance, duties, and any additional fees. A manufacturer in a country with duty advantages to your target market may have higher FOB pricing but lower total landed cost. For example, Bangladesh's duty-free access to EU markets can make higher FOB prices more economical than lower prices from China subject to 12% EU duty. Consider shipping time as well - faster shipping enables quicker inventory turns and lower holding costs.
      </p>

