  <!-- Section 6: Sample Process -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">The Sample Process | How to Evaluate Before Committing</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Samples prevent expensive bulk production mistakes. Never approve designs for production without physical samples. The sample process typically involves three stages: proto sample, fit sample, and pre-production sample. Understanding each stage and what to evaluate ensures you approve production only when quality meets your standards.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Proto Sample Stage</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        The proto sample is the first physical version of your design. Manufacturers create this sample to demonstrate they understand your specifications and can produce the garment. Evaluate the proto sample for overall design interpretation - does it match your vision? Check construction methods, fabric quality, and basic measurements. Proto samples often require revisions; this is normal. Provide specific feedback with measurements or photos where needed. The proto sample stage typically takes 7-14 days.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Fit Sample Stage</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Fit samples focus on sizing and proportion. After revising the proto sample, manufacturers produce fit samples in multiple sizes to evaluate grading and fit across your size range. Test fit samples on fit models or mannequins representing your target customer. Check that measurements match your size chart, proportions work across sizes, and the garment fits as intended. Fit issues identified at this stage are easier to fix than after bulk production. The fit sample stage adds another 7-14 days.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Pre-Production Sample Stage</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        The pre-production (PP) sample is the final version before bulk production. This sample should match production specifications exactly - correct fabric, accurate colors, proper construction, approved fit. The PP sample becomes the standard against which bulk production is measured. Evaluate this sample meticulously; check every seam, measurement, and detail. Once you approve the PP sample, the manufacturer proceeds to bulk cutting. This stage takes 7-14 days for final approval.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Normal Sampling Iterations</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Most projects require 2-3 sample rounds to achieve approval. One round is rare unless your design is very simple. Four or more rounds indicate communication problems or capability issues. If you reach four rounds with the same issues recurring, consider whether this manufacturer is the right fit. Excessive sampling costs money and delays launch. Set expectations upfront with the manufacturer about the number of included sample rounds and costs for additional iterations.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">When to Walk Away</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Walk away from the sampling process if you encounter these red flags: manufacturer cannot achieve basic specifications after 3 rounds, quality issues persist despite specific feedback, communication is poor or responses are delayed, manufacturer resists making requested changes, or sample quality significantly varies from what was promised. Sampling is your trial run - if it's difficult now, production will be worse. Cut losses and find a better manufacturing partner.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Sample Cost and Credit Policies</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Sample costs typically range from $50-200 per style depending on complexity. Most reputable manufacturers credit these costs against bulk orders, effectively making samples free when you proceed to production. Clarify the credit policy before starting - some deduct from final invoice, others apply as deposit credit. Ask whether sample costs are refundable if you decide not to proceed to production. SDF Clothing credits all sample costs against bulk orders to minimize your risk during the evaluation phase. Ensure you have professional tech packs ready - our <a href="/tech-pack-service">tech pack service</a> can help prepare detailed specifications.
      </p>

      <p class="section__text">
        For comprehensive sampling support, SDF Clothing provides efficient sample development with 7-14 day turnaround per round. We provide detailed feedback on technical feasibility and suggest optimizations to improve manufacturability. Our team has produced thousands of samples for brands globally and understands the importance of getting samples right before bulk production. Learn more about our <a href="/sampling-service">sampling service</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 7: Manufacturing Contracts -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Manufacturing Contracts | What to Include</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Contracts protect both parties by establishing clear expectations and remedies. While small orders may proceed without formal contracts, larger orders or ongoing relationships benefit from written agreements. Contracts need not be overly complex but should cover essential terms. Having clear documentation prevents misunderstandings and provides recourse if problems arise.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Product Specifications Reference</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        The contract should reference the approved tech pack, pre-production sample, and all specifications. Include fabric composition, weight, construction details, measurements with tolerances, color specifications with Pantone references, labeling requirements, and packaging specifications. These specifications form the quality standard for production and inspection. Ambiguity in specifications leads to disputes - be as specific as possible.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Quality Standards</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Define quality standards explicitly. Reference the approved sample as the quality benchmark. Specify acceptable quality level (AQL) | typically AQL 2.5 for major defects and AQL 4.0 for minor defects in garment manufacturing. Define defect allowances and replacement terms for goods exceeding these allowances. Include your right to inspection before shipment and procedures for addressing quality issues discovered after delivery. Establish responsibility for shipping damages.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Delivery Terms</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Specify Incoterms clearly - FOB (Free On Board), CIF (Cost, Insurance, Freight), or DDP (Delivered Duty Paid). FOB is most common for international manufacturing; you take ownership once goods load on the vessel. Include delivery timeline with key milestones: sample approval date, fabric readiness date, production start date, and shipment date. Include remedies for delays beyond reasonable causes such as force majeure events.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Payment Schedule</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Detail payment terms including deposit percentage (typically 30%), balance trigger (typically before shipment), and payment methods. Specify currency and any exchange rate provisions. Include provisions for price adjustments if specifications change significantly. For larger orders, consider using Letter of Credit which provides security for both parties. Clearly state when payments are due and any consequences for late payment.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">IP and Confidentiality Clauses</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Include intellectual property protection prohibiting the manufacturer from using your designs, patterns, or branding for other clients. Require return or destruction of all materials including patterns, samples, and documentation upon contract completion. Reference the separate NDA for additional confidentiality provisions. These clauses protect your brand identity and prevent manufacturers from producing your designs for competitors.
      </p>

      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Dispute Resolution</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Specify dispute resolution mechanisms including governing jurisdiction, mediation requirements, and arbitration procedures. International contracts should specify which country's laws apply and where disputes will be resolved. Many contracts specify arbitration in neutral locations like Singapore or Hong Kong for efficiency and neutrality. Clear dispute resolution procedures prevent costly litigation and provide a path forward if disagreements arise.
      </p>

      <p class="section__text">
        For additional protection, SDF Clothing provides standard contract templates covering all essential terms. We recommend having contracts reviewed by legal counsel familiar with international trade law. Clear documentation protects both parties and enables successful long-term partnerships.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

