  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">How to Find a Clothing Manufacturer FAQ</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Where can I find clothing manufacturers for my brand?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Five proven methods exist: direct outreach to country trade bodies like BGMEA for Bangladesh or AEPC for India (most reliable and free), Google search with specific terms like "knitwear factory Bangladesh" rather than generic searches, industry trade shows including Magic Las Vegas, Texworld NYC, and Canton Fair for face-to-face vetting, LinkedIn and industry networks for connecting with sourcing agents and factory representatives, and referrals from other brand founders which are often the most reliable source.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How do I know if a clothing manufacturer is legitimate?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Verify legitimacy through eight key checks: confirm business registration with government databases, verify certifications directly with issuing organizations like GOTS, OEKO-TEX, and BSCI, request third-party audit reports from organizations like WRAP or client-commissioned audits, contact client references from brands similar to yours, request factory photos or video tours showing actual production facilities, place a sample order to test quality and communication, review payment terms carefully avoiding 100% upfront demands, and ensure a signed contract with clear terms before proceeding.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What questions should I ask a clothing manufacturer?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Ask twelve critical questions: what is your minimum order quantity per style, can you share client references in my product category, what certifications do you hold, what is your standard sample process and timeline, do you charge for samples and is the cost credited against bulk orders, what are your payment terms, how do you handle quality issues before shipment, do you subcontract any production, what is your on-time delivery rate, can I visit your factory, do you have NDA or confidentiality agreements, and what is your reorder lead time.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How long does it take to find and start working with a manufacturer?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">The timeline typically ranges from 2 to 4 months for first-time brands. Research and initial outreach takes 2-4 weeks, receiving and comparing quotes requires 1-2 weeks, sample development and approval takes 3-6 weeks including multiple iterations, contract negotiation and deposit payment takes 1-2 weeks, and production lead time adds 40-45 days. Using trade body referrals or warm introductions can shorten this to 6-8 weeks, while cold outreach may extend to 4-6 months.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Should I use a sourcing agent to find a manufacturer?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Sourcing agents can be valuable for brands new to manufacturing or targeting unfamiliar countries. Agents provide local knowledge, language translation, factory vetting, and ongoing communication support. However, they add cost typically 5-15% of order value. For established brands or those comfortable with direct communication, working directly with manufacturers reduces costs. For first-time buyers or complex projects, a reputable <a href="/sourcing-agent-service">sourcing agent</a> can prevent costly mistakes and accelerate the process.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What is the minimum order quantity for most clothing manufacturers?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">MOQ varies significantly by region and manufacturer type. Bangladesh and India typically offer 300-500 piece MOQs for basic garments. China often requires 500-1,000 pieces but offers lower MOQs for repeat orders. Vietnam generally requires 500-1,500 pieces. Turkey and Portugal range from 300-1,000 pieces. USA and UK manufacturers typically require 100-300 pieces but at significantly higher costs. SDF Clothing offers 300 piece minimums per style, making us ideal for <a href="/clothing-manufacturers-for-startups">startup brands</a> testing new designs.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How do I protect my designs when approaching manufacturers?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Protect your designs through five key measures: register your trademark before approaching factories, use non-disclosure agreements when sharing detailed designs or tech packs, work with certified manufacturers like BGMEA members who have accountability structures, keep comprehensive tech packs on file as proof of original design, and consider registering distinctive design elements in key markets. SDF Clothing provides <a href="/confidentiality">confidentiality agreements</a> and respects intellectual property rights for all client projects.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">How do I compare quotes from different manufacturers?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Compare quotes across eight elements: FOB price per unit, fabric specifications ensuring identical materials across quotes, MOQ requirements and flexibility, lead time from deposit to shipment, payment terms and security, included services like sampling or quality inspection, certification status affecting retail access, and communication quality and responsiveness. The lowest price often cuts corners on quality or service - evaluate total value including reliability, not just unit cost.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">What is the best country to find clothing manufacturers?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">The best country depends on your priorities. Bangladesh offers lowest costs for basic garments with 300-piece MOQ and duty advantages for EU markets. China provides speed and technical capability with lower MOQs but higher costs. Vietnam balances cost and technical capability with duty advantages for US markets. Turkey offers proximity to Europe with quick turnaround. Portugal provides quality and speed for premium European brands. India offers competitive costs and cotton expertise. USA and UK provide communication ease and speed but at significantly higher costs. Choose based on product type, target market, budget, and timeline.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq__item">
          <h3 class="faq__question">Can startup brands find manufacturers willing to work with them?</h3>
          <p class="faq__answer">Yes, many manufacturers specialize in working with startup brands. Look for manufacturers advertising low MOQs, those explicitly mentioning startup support, and factories offering flexible terms. Bangladesh manufacturers like SDF Clothing built our business serving emerging brands with 300-piece minimums. Trade shows and online platforms like Maker's Row connect startups with manufacturer-friendly factories. Be prepared with professional communication, clear requirements, and realistic expectations. Start with 1-2 styles to build the relationship before expanding. Read our guide on <a href="/how-to-start-a-clothing-brand">how to start a clothing brand</a> for more tips.</p>
        </div>
